{{smaller|See ''allonge.''—'''Blenheim wig†''', a periwig: so named in honor of the battle of Blenheim (1704).—'''Campaign wig''', a wig used in traveling, with twisted side-locks and curled forehead. See 10 in cut on preceding page.—'''Cauliflower wig''', a variety of peruke in the eighteenth century, close curled, and covered with powder: so named from its supposed resemblance to a head of cauliflower when served at the table.—'''Welsh wig''', a worsted cap. ''Simmonds.''}}

'''wig'''<sup>3</sup> (wig), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''wigged'', ppr. ''wigging.'' [< ''wig''<sup>3</sup>, ''n.'', the orig. sense being perhaps ‘to put a wig on,’ i. e. to set right without ceremony, or ‘to snatch at (one’s) wig,’ to ruffle or handle (one) without ceremony. Compare ''wigging'', where the ref. to ''ear-wigging'' in the quot. is prob. humorous, the term meaning ‘wigging into one’s private ear,’ but alluding to ''earwig'', an annoying insect.] To rate or scold severely. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|If you wish to ’scape ''wigging'', a dumb wife ’s the dandy!
{{right|''Barham'', Ingoldsby Legends, II. 386.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
vätter, ''vätt'' = Dan. ''vætte'', an elf, = Goth. ''waihts'', f., ''waiht'', neut., a thing; prob. orig. ‘something moving’ (a moving object indistinctly seen at a distance, whether man, child, animal, elf, or demon), < AS. ''wegan'', etc., move, stir, carry: see ''weigh''<sup>1</sup>, ''wag''<sup>1</sup>. The word, by a phonetic change, also appears as mod. E. ''whit''<sup>1</sup>. It also appears unrecognized in ''aught'', ''naught'', ''not''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' A person, whether male or female; a human being: as, an unlucky ''wight''.
{{smaller block|There schulle thei fynde no ''Wight'' that will selle hem ony Vitaille or ony thing. {{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 130.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>To you, my purse, and to non other ''wight''
Compleyne I, for ye be my lady dere.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Complaint to his Purse, l. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>She was a ''wight'', if ever such ''wight'' were, . . .
To suckle fools and chronicle small beer.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, ii. 1. 159.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>No living ''wight'', save the Ladye alone,
Had dared to cross the threshold stone.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', L. of L. M., i. 1.}}}}
'''2†.''' A preternatural, unearthly, or uncanny creature; an elf, sprite, witch, or the like.
{{smaller block|<poem>“I crouche thee from elves and fro ''wightes'',”
Therwith the nyght-spel, seyde he anonrightes.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Miller’s Tale, l. 293.}}}}
'''3†.''' A space of time; a whit; a while.
{{smaller block|<poem>She was falle aslepe a litle ''wight''.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Reeve’s Tale, l. 363.}}}}

'''wight'''<sup>2</sup> (wīt), ''a.'' [< ME. ''wight'', ''wyght'', ''wicht'', ''wyte'', ''wiht'', ''wiȝt'', nimble, active, strong, < Icel. ''vígr'' (neut. ''vígt''), serviceable for war, in fighting condition (= Sw. ''vig'' (neut. ''vigt''), nimble, active, agile), < ''víg'' (= AS. ''wīg''), war; cf. ''vega'',

readings.c2s2:
fight, smite, Goth. ''weihan'', fight, strive, contend, L. ''vincere'', conquer: see ''victor'', ''vincible''. Cf. ''wie'', ''wye'', a warrior.] Having warlike prowess; valiant; courageous; strong and active; agile; nimble; swift. [Archaic.]
{{smaller block|<poem>He was a knight full kant, the kynges son of Lice,
And a ''wight'' mon in wer, wild of his dedis.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 6085.}}
<poem>I is ful ''wight'', God wat, as is a ra.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Reeve’s Tale, l. 166.}}
Le Balafré roared out for fair play, adding “that he would venture his nephew on him were he as ''wight'' as Wallace.”
{{right|''Scott'', Quentin Durward, xxxvii.}}}}

'''wight<sup>3</sup>†''', ''n.'' A Middle English form of ''weight''<sup>1</sup>.

'''wight<sup>4</sup>†''', ''n.'' See ''wite''<sup>1</sup>.

'''wightly†''' (wīt′li), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''wightly'', ''wihtliche'', ''wiȝtliche'', ''wiȝtli''; < ''wight''<sup>2</sup> + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] Swiftly; nimbly; quickly; vigorously; boldly.
{{smaller block|<poem>''Wiȝtliche'' with the child he went to his house,
and bi-tok it to his wif tiȝtly to kepe.</poem>
{{right|''William of Palerne'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 65.}}
<poem>Sho went vp ''wightly'' by a walle syde
To the toppe of a toure, & tot ouer the water
Ffor to loke on hir luffe, longyng in hert.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 862.}}
<poem>Ga ''wightly'' thou, and I sal keepe hym heere.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Reeve’s Tale, l. 182. (''Harl. MS.'')}}
<poem>For day that was is ''wightly'' past.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', Shep. Cal., September.}}}}

'''wightness†''' (wīt′nes), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wightnes''; < ''wight''<sup>2</sup> + ''-ness''.] Courage; vigor; bravery.
{{smaller block|<poem>Thurgh my ''wightnes'', I-wysse, & worthi Achilles,
We haue . . . getyn to the grekis this ground with oure help.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 12198.}}}}

