Indian, generally of a conical shape and formed of bark or mats, or now most often of skins,
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Wigwam.}}}}
laid over poles (called ''lodge-poles'') stacked on the ground and converging at the top, where is left an opening for the escape of smoke.
{{smaller block|Ye Indeans . . . departed from their ''wigwames.''
{{right|''Bradford'', Plymouth Plantation, p. 428.}}
Finch, of Watertown, had his ''wigwam'' burnt and all his goods.
{{right|''Winthrop'', Hist. New England, I. 43.}}
We then marched on, . . . and, falling upon several ''Wigwams'', burnt them.
{{right|''Coll. Mass. Hist. Soc.'' (1677), 2d ser., VIII. 142.}}}}
